  "sections": [
    {
      "id": "major-scales-advanced",
      "title": "Major Scales — Advanced",
      "level": 1,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "**Track:** theory | **Tier:** advanced | **Difficulty:** 6/10 | **Time:** ~35 min\n**Prerequisites:** Major Scales — Applied\n**Tags:** scales, modulation, tetrachords, analysis"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "overview",
      "title": "Overview",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Advanced tier: treat major scales as movable systems — modal mixture, brief tonicisation, and score analysis. You should already fluently build any major scale (applied tier) before this lesson."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "modal-mixture-and-borrowed-colour",
      "title": "Modal mixture and borrowed colour",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Major keys sometimes borrow **♭VI, ♭VII, ♭III** from the parallel minor for colour (e.g. in folk and rock). Recognising these as *scale-degree alterations* rather than \"wrong notes\" is an analysis skill built on secure major-scale degree naming."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "tonicisation-vs-modulation",
      "title": "Tonicisation vs modulation",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "**Tonicisation**: temporary emphasis of a non-tonic chord (often via secondary dominant — see harmony track).\n\n**Modulation**: established change of tonic. Major scales provide the target collection for the new key. Ear training: follow the pivot chord or sudden new accidental set."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "scale-networks",
      "title": "Scale networks",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Relative minor shares the same key signature (A minor ↔ C major). Parallel minor shares the same tonic (C minor ↔ C major). Advanced musicians flip between these views when arranging or improvising."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "representative-depth-not-exhaustive",
      "title": "Representative depth (not exhaustive)",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Know the **major scale as a system** — not every historical variant (musical modes, maqam, rag) which belong to regional units. This lesson closes the major-scale thread; modes get their own spiral in foundations and in Celtic/Irish regional study."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
