  "quiz_markdown": "### Q1. Parallel minor shares the same tonic as major.\n\n**Answer:** True\n\n### Q2. Relative minor shares the same key signature as its relative major.\n\n**Answer:** True\n\n### Q3. ♭VII in a major key is a borrowed scale degree from parallel minor.\n\n**Answer:** True\n\n### Q4. Tonicisation is temporary emphasis of a non-tonic key area.\n\n**Answer:** True\n\n### Q5. Modulation means a sustained change of tonic.\n\n**Answer:** True\n\n### Q6. C major and A minor are relative keys.\n\n**Answer:** True\n\n### Q7. C major and C minor are parallel keys.\n\n**Answer:** True\n\n### Q8. Secondary dominants belong to a later harmony tier.\n\n**Answer:** True\n\n### Q9. Advanced scale study supports score analysis more than first-time reading.\n\n**Answer:** True\n\n### Q10. Modal mixture adds colour without leaving major entirely.\n\n**Answer:** True\n\n### Q11. Pivot chords can smooth modulation between keys.\n\n**Answer:** True\n\n### Q12. Every world tradition uses WWHWWWH major — False globally, but True as Western major definition.\n\n**Answer:** False\n\n### Q13. Secure degree naming is prerequisite for borrowed-chord analysis.\n\n**Answer:** True\n\n### Q14. Regional units revisit scales in Irish, Arabic, and Indian contexts.\n\n**Answer:** True",
